today's video is going to be about my  experiences during undergrad studying  mathematics now I did do a double major  in physics and maths and I've already  made a video called my regret studying  physics so I thought it would only be  fair to make this one to start off I'll  tell you some things that I definitely  don't regret about studying maths number  one would be that I don't regret the  time that I spent studying so I don't  regret the time that I spent studying  and learning mathematical ideas instead  of anything else that I could have been  doing whether it's going out and  partying or doing any other activity I  don't regret that I spent a lot of that  time studying I also don't regret any of  the people that I've met whilst I've  been pursuing maths whether it was like  lecturers or students in my class and my  peers  I've really enjoyed some of the  friendships I've made through math  classes and in fact some of my best  friends have come from there so let's  talk about some of the things that I do  regret and this is actually pretty  nuanced because I would say overall I'm  glad I majored in mathematics I think  it's a good major and I definitely got a  lot out of it this is just inviting over  a captain hindsight to see a few things  that I guess I feel not the greatest  about so the first would be thinking  that I was good at math just because  I've done a course on it or because I  could get a good grade in that course I  also had a lot of confusion between  getting a good grade on something and  that meaning that I actually enjoyed it  what I mean is that I sort of felt like  sometimes more than I enjoyed maths I  just enjoyed the satisfaction of getting  a good grade on something or the feeling  that I could get from doing maths that  had nothing to do with maths at all and  that was more about my personal craving  to do something that satisfied me and to  get you know instant satisfaction in a  way that doing like a maths assignment  especially the low-hanging fruit or the  easy problems in math can give you you  know if you can apply some of these I  you've learned do the homework problem  handed in and get feedback pretty  quickly afterwards it kind of feels good  and it feels like you're accomplishing  something it feels good to just to be  able to finish something that's what I'm  especially talking about these sort of  low hanging fruit problems in math they  really gave me the sense of satisfaction  and I guess I enjoyed that I enjoyed  feeling like I was good at something and  I think it was good for my like self  confidence but I think I confused that  feeling I got from math with actually  enjoying it because sometimes I didn't  enjoy it at all but it was just masked  by this feeling that oh well I got a  good grade so I must be really enjoying  and I must be passionate about this  subject then sometimes I wasn't don't  get me wrong there are some areas of  math I really do enjoy for example I  found my dynamical systems course really  interesting but then there were some  courses that I took I think for other  reasons maybe one of those other reasons  was to like stroke my own ego like I  would take courses that I thought were  notoriously difficult just because I  felt like I should take that challenge  and that people taking those courses  will seem to me the best at math and and  maybe it was the perception that I  wanted other people to believe I was  good at maths as well so I feel like  maybe not all the choices I made in  terms of which classes I picked and  everything like that where I put my  effort was made based on what I actually  enjoyed but it was based on other things  it was based on what I thought I was  good at from these arbitrary grades and  measures and it was based on what I  thought was perceived as like the best  math to be doing or the most prestigious  math to be doing when really none of  those things met it as much so at the  time I wish I guess I'd been a little  bit more aware of what I actually  enjoyed and what I didn't my second  regret is probably thinking that some of  the math was more abstract than it  really was I was presented with a lot of  these really abstract ideas and math and  one thing about them is that they're  very hard I guess to show to people who  are  side of mathematics completely and have  them understand what's going on so it  kind of creates this feeling of like an  elite little Club of people who are in  this math class and they understand  something that kind of very few other  people understand and it's kind of a big  barrier to entry as well sometimes when  it comes to math so there were a lot of  these abstract ideas and I guess I  didn't ever really put much effort into  trying to communicate them to other  people and I would often maybe brush  them off as just being crazily complex  math ideas that you'd never understand  unless you're in this class but it also  affected my own thinking about these  ideas I guess sometimes I felt like  certain exercises and problems that I  was working on only existed in this  strange realm which consisted of the  lecturers in mind my notes and then on  the exam and the problems didn't seem to  exist anywhere outside of this and that  affected the way that I thought about  them I found them not so interesting or  important and I only studied for them in  a way that I was good at answering them  on the exam but didn't have much  knowledge about these problems to apply  outside of that context that lack of  understanding of the bigger picture of  some of these math ideas is one of my  regrets and actually I realized it from  an unlikely source and it was from a  Vsauce video I think the video I  remember it's called like the breakfast  okra and it's actually about finding the  quickest way that you can fall between  two points but the fact is that vsauce  made this really cool video about this  idea and linking it to all these other  physical ideas and it was really cool  video and I was thinking like I have  actually done lots of problems before on  the subject this register crow in math  class and physics class and I never  actually realized it was cool I never  thought about it as being interesting in  this context for other people and I kind  of realized that a lot of the ideas I've  studied were actually interesting  outside  the context of the exam but I've never  been able to see that before I've since  seen a lot of other videos it's someone  like three blue one brown presents math  ideas and I watch them and I think wow  this is actually interesting these ideas  are actually interesting these ideas  that I have learned about a uni but  never cared about I know I wished that  I'd seen a lot of these deeper meanings  at the time and actually used them to be  more engaged with what I was learning  again sure I did appreciate some things  I mean I managed to get through the math  degree so I had some amount of  inspiration behind me and what I'm doing  now on YouTube I think you can see that  I do have a passion for math somewhere  down there but it's definitely been very  small at times and has come and gone but  I really feel like it wasn't until after  I finished a lot of these courses that I  realized these ideas were actually cool  and that I probably should have spent  more time trying to understand how I  could communicate them to other people  because it would have helped me  appreciate that they were cool so those  are my thoughts here it's just based on  my personal experience so let me know if  you can relate to any of these ideas and  as always thanks for watching
 

